WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.245 --> 00:00:16.416
I've seen Koreans comedians
come on and try to be funny

00:00:16.449 --> 00:00:18.551
while putting black
shoe polish on their--

00:00:18.584 --> 00:00:20.320
That's not acceptable.

00:00:21.854 --> 00:00:25.091
Living here,
I have never felt the need

00:00:25.124 --> 00:00:26.292
to be afraid of the police.

00:00:26.325 --> 00:00:28.161
One thing that I really
love about Korea

00:00:28.194 --> 00:00:31.064
and that would keep me here
is the safety.

00:00:31.097 --> 00:00:33.633
( speaking Korean )
Oh, is this about dating?

00:00:33.666 --> 00:00:35.635
( speaking Korean )
Dating.

00:00:35.668 --> 00:00:37.503 align:start size:78% position:22%
- It's not friend?
- No, no.

00:00:37.536 --> 00:00:39.338
- Come on in, bruh.
- Yeah, let's go!

00:00:39.371 --> 00:00:41.207
- Come on over.
- ( laughing )

00:00:41.240 --> 00:00:42.575
My name is Tanaya.

00:00:42.608 --> 00:00:44.077
- Man1ac.
- Shakerra.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:45.511
- Tina.
- Sam.

00:00:45.544 --> 00:00:47.346
( speaking Korean )
My name is Han Hyun Min,

00:00:47.379 --> 00:00:49.282
and I am Black in Korea.

00:00:49.315 --> 00:00:51.350
I learned so much
from you guys today.

00:00:51.383 --> 00:00:54.787 align:start size:88% position:13%
- New community.
- New adventures
  with our new friends.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:00:57.457
( speaking Korean )
Cheers!

00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:03.563
Narrator:<i> South Korea
 is best known for its food,</i>

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.832
<i> technology,
 and entertainment,</i>

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:09.368
<i> including the global
 phenomenon K-pop.</i>

00:01:09.401 --> 00:01:12.371
<i> But when it comes
 to fashion and music,</i>

00:01:12.404 --> 00:01:14.807
<i> South Korea has had
 a lot of influence</i>

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:16.342
<i> from African-American culture.</i>

00:01:16.375 --> 00:01:18.811
<i> ( music playing )</i>

00:01:18.844 --> 00:01:23.416
<i> This influence dates back
 to the 1950s</i>

00:01:23.449 --> 00:01:27.653
<i> when the US sent forces
 into South Korea
 during the Korean war,</i>

00:01:27.686 --> 00:01:31.224
<i> including many Black people
 who decided to settle there.</i>

00:01:31.257 --> 00:01:34.861
<i> Today, South Korea's
 Black community is made up
 of Africans</i>

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:38.397
<i> and African-Americans who
 eventually decided to stay</i>

00:01:38.430 --> 00:01:40.399
<i> after completing
 their military service</i>

00:01:40.432 --> 00:01:43.402
<i> as well as scholars
 and entrepreneurs.</i>

00:01:43.435 --> 00:01:45.404
<i> Within the last 30 years,</i>

00:01:45.437 --> 00:01:47.573
<i> the affinity
 for Black culture has grown</i>

00:01:47.606 --> 00:01:51.811
<i> across South Korea's
 99% ethnic Korean population,</i>

00:01:51.844 --> 00:01:54.413
<i> and the influence
 continues to grow</i>

00:01:54.446 --> 00:01:57.316
<i> as Black people
 from all over the world</i>

00:01:57.349 --> 00:01:58.885
<i> have made South Korea
 their home.</i>

00:01:58.918 --> 00:02:01.521
<i> For this reason,</i>

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:04.423
<i> we brought together
 a spectrum of Black people
 living in Korea</i>

00:02:04.456 --> 00:02:06.893
<i> to share their unique stories
 about culture</i>

00:02:06.926 --> 00:02:10.830
<i> community, and the Black
 experience in Korea.</i>

00:02:10.863 --> 00:02:14.300
<i> ( theme music playing )</i>

00:02:27.313 --> 00:02:30.650 line:0%
Man: Three, two, one.

00:02:39.992 --> 00:02:44.297 line:0%
I feel like my style
has maybe, like, leveled up

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:46.299 line:0%
since I came to Korea,
'cause before

00:02:46.332 --> 00:02:48.367
I was very much of, like,
a tomgirl, whatever.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:51.470
But I know that, like--
you know, go to Walmart
with some sweatpants on.

00:02:51.503 --> 00:02:53.306
- ( overlapping chatter )
- You know, no makeup on.
  You know what I mean?

00:02:53.339 --> 00:02:55.441
- Why you stop doing that?
- They just so comfortable.

00:02:55.474 --> 00:02:57.610
But here in Korea,
everyone's always pretty.

00:02:57.643 --> 00:02:58.945
They have their makeup on.

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:00.646
I'm like,
oh, I'm going to go to my--

00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:03.482 align:start position:0%
- Let me put some eyelashes on.
- ( overlapping chatter )

00:03:03.515 --> 00:03:05.618
Some cute pants on.
Let me put on the cute outfits.

00:03:05.651 --> 00:03:08.487 line:0%
I used to show cleavage
for days back in LA,

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:10.623 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- but in Korea, it's...
- ( laughter )

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:12.992
...not really, unless
it's a Friday, Saturday night.

00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:16.329
And even then
you got to cover up
on the train a little bit

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:17.830
so that they're not
staring at you.

00:03:17.863 --> 00:03:19.966
So, yeah,
that's why I came over here.

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:22.835
I have to cover up
a little bit more
than I would in LA.

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:25.504 line:0%
( speaking Korean )
When I travel internationally,

00:03:25.537 --> 00:03:27.840 line:0%
I'm mesmerized by people having
their own individual style.

00:03:27.873 --> 00:03:31.677
Remember in winter
when people only wore
those long puff jackets?

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:35.815
Everyone owned one.
I didn't see the beauty
in those.

00:03:35.848 --> 00:03:38.351
( speaking Korean )
But back in school,

00:03:38.384 --> 00:03:39.752
weren't you bullied for not
wearing the long puff jacket?

00:03:39.785 --> 00:03:42.755
Like, "Oh, you can't afford
to buy one!"

00:03:42.788 --> 00:03:44.857
( speaking Korean )
That wasn't necessary because
at the end of the day

00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:46.892
I could just wear
something better.

00:03:46.925 --> 00:03:48.995
( speaking Korean )
I mean, you can!

00:03:49.028 --> 00:03:52.632
You definitely can because
you're handsome and a model!

00:03:52.665 --> 00:03:55.735
( speaking Korean )
If you have your own style,
people respect you for that.

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:57.870
But if you wear
what everyone else wears,
you're the same,

00:03:57.903 --> 00:04:00.740
and I don't want to be the same.

00:04:00.773 --> 00:04:02.308 line:0%
I have a certain
body type here

00:04:02.341 --> 00:04:04.277 line:0%
that clothes are not
always accommodated for,

00:04:04.310 --> 00:04:08.581
so I find myself having to
make my clothes fit
for the culture.

00:04:08.614 --> 00:04:10.850
I'm still wearing the clothes
I would back home...

00:04:10.883 --> 00:04:13.552 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Right.
- ...but I'm having
  to make it fit

00:04:13.585 --> 00:04:16.589
into what
is socially acceptable.

00:04:18.757 --> 00:04:22.928
I've been living in Korea
permanently since 2019.

00:04:22.961 --> 00:04:24.697
I like living here in Korea,

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:28.301
<i> but I feel that
 beauty standards in Korea</i>

00:04:28.334 --> 00:04:30.636
<i> cater towards
 white skin, fair hair,</i>

00:04:30.669 --> 00:04:36.642 line:0%
and anything that revolves
around being skinny.

00:04:36.675 --> 00:04:39.745
<i> Sometimes
 I'll go into the stalls</i>

00:04:39.778 --> 00:04:42.481
<i> where they sell the clothes
 on the side of the streets</i>

00:04:42.514 --> 00:04:44.750
and the ladies will look at me,

00:04:44.783 --> 00:04:48.387
and they're like,
"Girl, you got too much
junk back there.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:50.056
<i> None of these clothes
 are going to fit you."</i>

00:04:50.089 --> 00:04:51.957
<i> And I appreciate the honesty,</i>

00:04:51.990 --> 00:04:53.693
but sometimes
I just want to look.

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:56.729
<i> It's unfortunate,
 because I feel that</i>

00:04:56.762 --> 00:05:00.766
<i> there are also
 Korean people who have
 similar body types as me</i>

00:05:00.799 --> 00:05:03.402
<i> or who have dark skin
 just like me.</i>

00:05:03.435 --> 00:05:08.607
And I can only imagine
how they feel being
that they're Korean

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:11.978
and they're probably not
as accepted either.

00:05:20.018 --> 00:05:22.455
Man: Three, two, one.

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:31.831
Consensus.

00:05:31.864 --> 00:05:35.101 line:0%
Listen, we're on the same line.

00:05:35.134 --> 00:05:39.972
So, I feel like
one of the best ways
to learn a language

00:05:40.005 --> 00:05:42.441
is to actually immerse
yourself in the culture.

00:05:42.474 --> 00:05:46.112
And if one of those ways is
actually being with somebody,

00:05:46.145 --> 00:05:47.546
although it's not
the main reason,

00:05:47.579 --> 00:05:49.715
it's one of the ways
that can help.

00:05:49.748 --> 00:05:53.819
And I feel like it's
a beneficial part on both ends
of the relationship.

00:05:53.852 --> 00:05:55.821
Because it's always Korean,
it's also English.

00:05:55.854 --> 00:05:58.657 line:0%
I mean, for me personally,
when I first came to Korea,

00:05:58.690 --> 00:06:01.660 line:0%
and I always asked people
what's the best way
to learn the language,

00:06:01.693 --> 00:06:04.663
they always say get yourself
a Korean girlfriend.

00:06:04.696 --> 00:06:06.499 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah.
- It's the quickest way

00:06:06.532 --> 00:06:08.734
because you spend a lot
of time with her.

00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:11.837
But I realized, okay,
I could also spend time
with just friends.

00:06:11.870 --> 00:06:14.840
If you find a bunch of friends
who are really eager
to study Korean,

00:06:14.873 --> 00:06:17.510
that can really help you a lot,
at least for my experience.

00:06:17.543 --> 00:06:19.645
Especially foreigners
that don't speak English.

00:06:19.678 --> 00:06:20.980
- Right, right, right.
- And then you can only
  communicate in Korean.

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:22.115
- That's true.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:24.917 --> 00:06:28.754 line:0%
My name's Tanaya,
I have a tech company out here.

00:06:28.787 --> 00:06:31.056
<i> Been living in Korea
 for five years,</i>

00:06:31.089 --> 00:06:33.159
( indistinct chatter )

00:06:33.192 --> 00:06:34.827
<i> I feel like only in Korea</i>

00:06:34.860 --> 00:06:38.030
could I have ever become
a tech CEO in my twenties.

00:06:38.063 --> 00:06:41.600
<i> I've had a very strong
 dating life in Korea.</i>

00:06:41.633 --> 00:06:45.137
<i> Right now, I've been seeing
someone for the past two years.</i>

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:48.207
My sweetheart and I,
we actually have
a pretty good dynamic.

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:50.843
<i> I think what helped a lot
 with our relationship</i>

00:06:50.876 --> 00:06:53.712
<i> is that he didn't really
 grow up watching TV,</i>

00:06:53.745 --> 00:06:55.881
so he really didn't have
any preconceptions

00:06:55.914 --> 00:06:58.017
of what someone who looks
like me should be

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.819
or what Americans should be.

00:06:59.852 --> 00:07:02.721
<i> He's not concerned
 what other people think.</i>

00:07:02.754 --> 00:07:05.591
He just likes me for me.
Of course, I like him for him.

00:07:05.624 --> 00:07:08.194
I'm curious, Hyun Min,
what do you think?

00:07:08.227 --> 00:07:12.031
( speaking Korean )
Have you dated someone
in order to learn English?

00:07:12.064 --> 00:07:14.633
( speaking Korean )
Oh, is this about dating?

00:07:14.666 --> 00:07:16.836
( speaking Korean )
Dating.

00:07:16.869 --> 00:07:18.571 align:start size:78% position:22%
- It's not friend?
- No, no.

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:22.141
- Come on in, bruh.
- Yeah, let's go!

00:07:22.174 --> 00:07:23.642
( laughter )

00:07:23.675 --> 00:07:26.779
( overlapping chatter )

00:07:31.617 --> 00:07:34.587 line:0%
Man: Three, two, one.

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:37.223 align:start size:72% position:28% line:0%
- Whee.
- Hell, yeah!

00:07:37.256 --> 00:07:39.058 line:0%
- How you doing?
- Mm-hmm, yeah.

00:07:39.091 --> 00:07:41.193 line:0%
- Who's here?
- Let's see. Okay.

00:07:41.226 --> 00:07:45.231 line:0%
I can't stand there
'cause acceptance from
the Black community.

00:07:45.264 --> 00:07:48.934
When I'm in the states,
they call me Chinese Mike.
You know what I'm saying?

00:07:48.967 --> 00:07:50.736 align:start size:88% position:13%
- 'Cause I-- I tell them
  I'm half Asian.
- Right, right.

00:07:50.769 --> 00:07:52.605
But you clearly sound like
a Black man, though, like--

00:07:52.638 --> 00:07:54.240 line:0%
I mean, I can sound like it,
but I can--

00:07:54.273 --> 00:07:56.242 line:0%
I mean, it doesn't mean
I'm accepted as one.

00:07:56.275 --> 00:07:58.611 line:0%
Which makes me think, am I?
You know what I'm saying?

00:07:58.644 --> 00:08:00.613 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Oh, I see it.
- I know my bloodline,

00:08:00.646 --> 00:08:03.082
but since I'm half-Korean
it makes me, like--

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:06.852
I'd be standing over there
if it wasn't for the objections
of the Black community.

00:08:06.885 --> 00:08:08.954
Being biracial,
you guys kind of are stuck

00:08:08.987 --> 00:08:11.957
in between this weird,
like, am I Korean?

00:08:11.990 --> 00:08:14.260
Am I Black? Where do I fit in?
Where's my community?

00:08:14.293 --> 00:08:17.029
But you are also biracial,
too, so...

00:08:17.062 --> 00:08:18.797
Oh, yeah, well,
'cause I'm Black and Mexican,

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:21.133
but, like, all of us came out
Black, so it's like...

00:08:21.166 --> 00:08:24.103 align:start size:84% position:16%
- ( laughing )
- I mean, like--
  so it is what it is.

00:08:24.136 --> 00:08:26.972
And it was interesting,
'cause the Mexicans
in our family

00:08:27.005 --> 00:08:28.741 line:0%
didn't want Blacks
in their family,

00:08:28.774 --> 00:08:30.809 line:0%
so I didn't really get to
partake in much of that culture.

00:08:30.842 --> 00:08:33.746
So, yeah, that's why
I'm just black-ety Black.

00:08:33.779 --> 00:08:35.814
I am a Black woman.

00:08:35.847 --> 00:08:38.284
( speaking Korean )
Well, I'm half Asian,

00:08:38.317 --> 00:08:44.290
and something a lot of people
ask me when I'm in the U.S.

00:08:44.323 --> 00:08:47.059
is "You're not full Black,
are you?"

00:08:47.092 --> 00:08:53.065
Or they say,
"You're biracial, huh?"

00:08:53.098 --> 00:08:58.170
So I believe I consider myself
half and half.

00:08:58.203 --> 00:08:59.338
50/50.

00:08:59.371 --> 00:09:01.874
I'm 70/30.

00:09:05.611 --> 00:09:09.181
( speaking Korean )
My mom is Korean

00:09:09.214 --> 00:09:13.218
and my father
is from Africa-- Nigerian.

00:09:13.251 --> 00:09:18.090
<i> Before and during
 kindergarten,</i>

00:09:18.123 --> 00:09:22.328
<i> my parents took care of me...</i>

00:09:23.895 --> 00:09:26.131
...so I didn't consider
myself different.

00:09:26.164 --> 00:09:28.033
<i> But after entering school,</i>

00:09:28.066 --> 00:09:31.103
<i> many kids looked at me
 like I was weird,</i>

00:09:31.136 --> 00:09:35.341
and I was often picked on.

00:09:35.374 --> 00:09:37.343
<i> When I was young,</i>

00:09:37.376 --> 00:09:40.012
<i> there weren't many
 mixed kids like me...</i>

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:49.922
...and people
had bad notions about it.

00:09:49.955 --> 00:09:55.761
But nowadays, I think that
perception is changing.

00:10:02.934 --> 00:10:06.071
Man: Three, two, one.

00:10:06.104 --> 00:10:08.374 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- ( muffled chatter )
- Offended is such
  a strong word.

00:10:08.407 --> 00:10:10.976 line:0%
Yeah, I know. Strong, right?

00:10:11.009 --> 00:10:13.646 line:0%
- Offended is such
  a strong word.
- Offended. Yeesh.

00:10:13.679 --> 00:10:17.383 line:0%
Sometimes I feel like
in some instances,

00:10:17.416 --> 00:10:20.786 line:0%
it comes from a place where
they are trying to learn.

00:10:20.819 --> 00:10:22.921 align:start position:0%
- Right.
- But we also have to understand

00:10:22.954 --> 00:10:26.091
appropriation can lead
to negative stereotypes

00:10:26.124 --> 00:10:30.229
because sometimes it feels
like a cosplay of Black people.

00:10:30.262 --> 00:10:34.833
There are also people out there
who are literally putting on
Black people as a costume.

00:10:34.866 --> 00:10:38.103
At the end of the day,
I'm always going to
be like this.

00:10:38.136 --> 00:10:40.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
- I can't-- so it's like...
- Mm, mm. Feel you.

00:10:40.305 --> 00:10:42.775 line:0%
- That makes sense, yeah.
- Say you like rock and--

00:10:42.808 --> 00:10:44.943 line:0%
I mean, say you like rock
and roll or something.

00:10:44.976 --> 00:10:47.413 align:start size:84% position:16% line:0%
- But there's an image
  to that genre.
- Right, I get it.

00:10:47.446 --> 00:10:49.815 align:start size:88% position:13%
- They want to be a part
  of that genre.
- Right.

00:10:49.848 --> 00:10:51.950 align:start size:97% position:3%
- So, how can you--
  I don't find that offensive.
- Right.

00:10:51.983 --> 00:10:53.952
If that I see that, I'm like,
it makes me feel good.

00:10:53.985 --> 00:10:57.056
I'd rather them
want to be us than--
you know what I'm saying?

00:10:57.089 --> 00:10:58.424 line:0%
Yeah, I get what you're saying.
At the end of the day,

00:10:58.457 --> 00:10:59.958 line:0%
when you live within
a certain culture,

00:10:59.991 --> 00:11:01.326 line:0%
you want to emulate
the good parts,

00:11:01.359 --> 00:11:02.995 line:0%
the good aspects
of that culture, right?

00:11:03.028 --> 00:11:06.065
If you like Michael Jordan
and you wore a jersey

00:11:06.098 --> 00:11:08.934
that said 23, I know
this guy likes Michael.

00:11:08.967 --> 00:11:12.304
You don't have to go
above and beyond and paint
your face and try to do--

00:11:12.337 --> 00:11:15.007
Like that?
That I'm like, nah,
that ain't it.

00:11:15.040 --> 00:11:18.811
So it almost goes
from you appreciating it
to almost, like, mocking it.

00:11:18.844 --> 00:11:21.814
Yeah, there's a difference
between mocking
the Black culture

00:11:21.847 --> 00:11:24.149
and trying to make
their self look like you.

00:11:24.182 --> 00:11:26.985
Like, when I was younger,
I've seen comedians,

00:11:27.018 --> 00:11:29.455
like, Korean comedians,
come on and try to be funny

00:11:29.488 --> 00:11:33.826
while putting
black shoe polish on their--
that's not acceptable.

00:11:33.859 --> 00:11:37.229
But I don't think nobody
who put dreads in their hair,
they hated the culture.

00:11:37.262 --> 00:11:38.864 align:start size:94% position:6%
- You know what I'm saying?
- Right.

00:11:38.897 --> 00:11:41.834
That's how I feel.
If it's the paint thing, no.

00:11:41.867 --> 00:11:45.904 line:0%
I was going to do
this movie shoot and one
of the directors was like,

00:11:45.937 --> 00:11:48.841
"Hey, do you know where
I can get some fake hair
to do some cornrows?"

00:11:48.874 --> 00:11:50.476
And I was like, "For me?

00:11:50.509 --> 00:11:52.344
I don't want cornrows
in my hair.

00:11:52.377 --> 00:11:53.846
Are we just talking
about braids or what?"

00:11:53.879 --> 00:11:56.849
She's like,
"No, for an actual actor."

00:11:56.882 --> 00:11:59.852
And I was like, "Is he Black?"

00:11:59.885 --> 00:12:01.353
And she's like,
"No, he's Korean."

00:12:01.386 --> 00:12:03.021
So, for me, I don't
necessarily get offended.

00:12:03.054 --> 00:12:04.457
I just want to be
the educator.

00:12:06.925 --> 00:12:10.028 line:0%
While, I'm really thankful
to be here in Korea

00:12:10.061 --> 00:12:13.866 line:0%
because I come from such
a small town in Tennessee.

00:12:13.899 --> 00:12:15.868
<i> So I never thought
 that I would actually</i>

00:12:15.901 --> 00:12:19.772
<i> be living my K-pop
 choreographer dream.</i>

00:12:19.805 --> 00:12:23.375
Korean people normally
think that I'm very cool
when they see me,

00:12:23.408 --> 00:12:27.112
but they also think
that I am very intimidating.

00:12:27.145 --> 00:12:30.516
When people tell me that,
I always question, like, why?

00:12:30.549 --> 00:12:33.886
I'm such a nice person.
I'm very bubbly.

00:12:33.919 --> 00:12:36.522
<i> But I can understand
 because I'm different.</i>

00:12:36.555 --> 00:12:41.527
<i> You know, like, they've
 never really seen someone of
 a difference race before.</i>

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:45.998
<i> And then also
 there's the stereotypes
 of Black people,</i>

00:12:46.031 --> 00:12:48.834
and then maybe
they're also intimidated
by my presence.

00:12:54.339 --> 00:12:56.942
Man: Three, two, one.

00:12:56.975 --> 00:13:00.012 line:0%
( overlapping chatter )

00:13:00.045 --> 00:13:02.915 align:start size:78% position:22% line:0%
- ( all laughing )
- Oh, I agree!

00:13:02.948 --> 00:13:05.250 line:0%
( overlapping chatter )

00:13:05.283 --> 00:13:08.086 line:0%
- Absolutely, like!
- Shut your mouth!

00:13:08.119 --> 00:13:11.423 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- Watch your mouth on TV.
- I have been unemployed
  for two years, bro!

00:13:11.456 --> 00:13:13.525 line:0%
I know. Sam can tell it best.

00:13:13.558 --> 00:13:15.027 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- Go ahead, Sam, preach.
- Okay, Sam, please,

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:18.030
please explain to us
your situation.

00:13:18.063 --> 00:13:21.433 line:0%
Never in my wildest dreams
did I ever think

00:13:21.466 --> 00:13:23.268 line:0%
that speaking on something
that I felt like

00:13:23.301 --> 00:13:28.941
I was entitled to speak on
would get me canceled so hard.

00:13:28.974 --> 00:13:33.278
Like, literally, when I made
the post about blackface,

00:13:33.311 --> 00:13:35.948
it became a topic overnight.

00:13:35.981 --> 00:13:39.284
I couldn't do any work.
Like, they canceled me hard.

00:13:39.317 --> 00:13:41.453 align:start position:0%
- Dropped me like a hot potato.
- Oh, my God.

00:13:41.486 --> 00:13:44.122 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Yeah, man.
- But here's what gets me,
  is that you had been

00:13:44.155 --> 00:13:46.291 align:start size:84% position:16%
- talking about racism
  before in Korea.
- Exactly, yes.

00:13:46.324 --> 00:13:48.594
So what do you think
made this last time different?

00:13:48.627 --> 00:13:52.297
When your platform
gets bigger, right, you have
more responsibility.

00:13:52.330 --> 00:13:56.301
And so what you say really
affects a lot of people, right?

00:13:56.334 --> 00:13:59.972
And I'm sure
at a certain point, they're
like, "Oh, this is Sam,

00:14:00.005 --> 00:14:02.307
the Black guy in Korea
who's doing entertainment,"
right?

00:14:02.340 --> 00:14:04.610
I'm always talking good
about Kor-- people like that.

00:14:04.643 --> 00:14:08.580
But the one time
that I said something that was
perceived as negative,

00:14:08.613 --> 00:14:09.948
they were like,
"Nah, you can't do that."

00:14:09.981 --> 00:14:11.316 align:start size:81% position:19%
- They X'd you out.
- Exactly.

00:14:11.349 --> 00:14:13.151
They felt like
it was an attack on them

00:14:13.184 --> 00:14:15.988
and they X'd me out hard,
to the point where

00:14:16.021 --> 00:14:18.323
my own Korean friends
that supported me,

00:14:18.356 --> 00:14:20.292 align:start size:97% position:3%
- they were getting attacked.
- ( gasping )

00:14:20.325 --> 00:14:21.627
( speaking Korean )
To be honest,

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:24.630
I feel like to be
a public figure in Korea

00:14:24.663 --> 00:14:27.199
means that
you really have to watch out
for a lot of things.

00:14:27.232 --> 00:14:31.003
I'm super careful
with my social media,

00:14:31.036 --> 00:14:34.373
which kind of keeps me away
from using it personally.

00:14:34.406 --> 00:14:36.909
( speaking Korean )
When you go up the ladder,
it takes place slowly,

00:14:36.942 --> 00:14:39.545 align:start size:91% position:9%
- but going down
  takes place quite fast.
- Skydive.

00:14:41.212 --> 00:14:43.315
That is facts.

00:14:47.285 --> 00:14:51.356
( speaking Korean )
Have a good evening! Thank you.

00:14:51.389 --> 00:14:54.092
<i> I work in Korea as an actor
 and a TV personality.</i>

00:14:54.125 --> 00:14:55.961
( speaking Korean )
Are you visiting
for Thanksgiving?

00:14:55.994 --> 00:14:57.663
<i> I love this place so much,
 everywhere I went,</i>

00:14:57.696 --> 00:14:59.698
<i> I used my platform
 to promote Korea.</i>

00:14:59.731 --> 00:15:03.002 line:0%
I have experienced
a lot of good things
compared to bad things.

00:15:05.170 --> 00:15:07.673
So, Sam, what is this called?

00:15:07.706 --> 00:15:10.676
This is called
shrimp extraordinaire.

00:15:10.709 --> 00:15:15.080 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Oh, okay.
- Shrimp extraordinaire. Yeah!

00:15:15.113 --> 00:15:16.315
So, I grew up in Ghana.

00:15:16.348 --> 00:15:18.517
<i> Accra, to be precise.</i>

00:15:18.550 --> 00:15:21.219
<i> I have five younger siblings.
 And I used to cook.</i>

00:15:21.252 --> 00:15:23.322
<i> I was the designated cook
 of my family.</i>

00:15:23.355 --> 00:15:25.223
My younger siblings
are getting older now,

00:15:25.256 --> 00:15:28.060
<i> and it's really difficult
 to connect,</i>

00:15:28.093 --> 00:15:29.962
and it can get pretty
lonely sometimes.

00:15:29.995 --> 00:15:33.565
When I look back and I reflect,
two years ago, right?

00:15:33.598 --> 00:15:36.234
I really wonder
what would've happened

00:15:36.267 --> 00:15:39.404
if I didn't have friends
who stood, like, beside me.

00:15:39.437 --> 00:15:41.740 align:start size:84% position:16%
- You guys were, like,
  ready to go to war.
- Oh, yeah.

00:15:41.773 --> 00:15:45.744
<i> In August of 2020,
 I experienced cancel culture.</i>

00:15:45.777 --> 00:15:47.245
People kept sending me a post

00:15:47.278 --> 00:15:51.049
about a group of students
that did cosplay

00:15:51.082 --> 00:15:53.051
but they painted
their faces black.

00:15:53.084 --> 00:15:54.553
<i> I went on the 'Gram</i>

00:15:54.586 --> 00:15:57.255
<i> talking about
 how disappointed I was.</i>

00:15:57.288 --> 00:16:01.159
<i> But somehow things blew up.</i>

00:16:01.192 --> 00:16:03.428
and the hate that poured in
was so overwhelming

00:16:03.461 --> 00:16:05.130
I did not know what to expect.

00:16:05.163 --> 00:16:07.032
The truth is, it can be
too much for one person.

00:16:07.065 --> 00:16:09.267
You guys, and there were
some of my Korean friends

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:12.104
who were like,
"Nah, we're going to
stick beside you."

00:16:12.137 --> 00:16:13.472
That really meant a lot to me,

00:16:13.505 --> 00:16:16.108
and for me that was the moment
where I realized

00:16:16.141 --> 00:16:17.609
these guys are not
just my friends,

00:16:17.642 --> 00:16:19.611
these guys are like family.

00:16:27.419 --> 00:16:29.621
Man: Three, two, one.

00:16:34.159 --> 00:16:37.496 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- Uh, yeah...
- I don't know if I should
  stand here or here.

00:16:37.529 --> 00:16:39.464
So the po-po came
for y'all or...

00:16:39.497 --> 00:16:42.701 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I'm super curious
  to know what happened.
- ( imitating siren )

00:16:42.734 --> 00:16:44.469
- Like, what happened?
- I used to party a lot.

00:16:44.502 --> 00:16:46.705 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Right.
- And since I speak Korean,

00:16:46.738 --> 00:16:49.541
I hear everything
that they're saying,

00:16:49.574 --> 00:16:51.443
especially when they speak
about Black people.

00:16:51.476 --> 00:16:53.412 line:0%
'Cause that was the crowd
I used to--

00:16:53.445 --> 00:16:55.714 line:0%
you know, the military soldiers
I used to kick it with.

00:16:55.747 --> 00:17:00.585
But, yeah, they actually said
the n-word and...

00:17:00.618 --> 00:17:03.121
Did they realize
that you're also Black?
Or they don't know?

00:17:03.154 --> 00:17:06.324
They didn't-- well, they knew--
yeah, we was all dark.

00:17:06.357 --> 00:17:09.227
We were all sitting there.
Like, everybody was Black.
You know what I'm saying?

00:17:09.260 --> 00:17:11.496
But they said it
in Korean at first

00:17:11.529 --> 00:17:13.165
and then they said it
in English.

00:17:13.198 --> 00:17:15.500 align:start size:78% position:22%
- Mm.
- So, I-- I mean,

00:17:15.533 --> 00:17:17.335
then we got into
a physical altercation.

00:17:17.368 --> 00:17:20.505
That's when it popped off.
Yeah, I hurt a couple of people.

00:17:20.538 --> 00:17:23.175
See, that's crazy
because living here,

00:17:23.208 --> 00:17:26.511
I have never felt the need
to be afraid of the police.

00:17:26.544 --> 00:17:29.815 line:0%
It's so safe. Every time
I see the police just strolling

00:17:29.848 --> 00:17:32.350
around in the neighborhood,
just say hi to you.

00:17:32.383 --> 00:17:36.688
And I'm like, yo,
you can never feel like this
anywhere else.

00:17:36.721 --> 00:17:38.824
Like when I went
to America the first time

00:17:38.857 --> 00:17:41.793
and I was driving,
a police stopped me.

00:17:41.826 --> 00:17:43.695 align:start size:81% position:19%
- And I was scared!
- Of course.

00:17:43.728 --> 00:17:45.530
Out of my-- I was like,
oh, my gosh.

00:17:45.563 --> 00:17:47.199
I had to pretend not
to speak English.

00:17:47.232 --> 00:17:49.367
- So I spoke Korean.
- How did you do that?

00:17:49.400 --> 00:17:51.837 align:start size:91% position:9%
- How'd you pull that off?
- I spoke Korean
  and I showed them

00:17:51.870 --> 00:17:53.805
my Korean driver's license.

00:17:53.838 --> 00:17:56.708
And they looked--
like, "Ah, dang."

00:17:56.741 --> 00:17:58.210
And they just let me go.

00:17:58.243 --> 00:18:00.545 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Nice.
- But I was scared!

00:18:00.578 --> 00:18:03.115
I was really scared.
But I never felt that here.

00:18:03.148 --> 00:18:07.219
And I got to say, man,
I really feel safe.

00:18:07.252 --> 00:18:09.421
Yeah, without a doubt,
definitely living here.

00:18:16.227 --> 00:18:19.131
Man: Three, two, one.

00:18:19.164 --> 00:18:24.469 line:0%
( all groaning )

00:18:24.502 --> 00:18:26.105
( groans )

00:18:27.672 --> 00:18:29.741
You know what?
I'm gonna go with agree.

00:18:29.774 --> 00:18:32.811 align:start size:81% position:19%
- Yeah, I'm going to
  stick to this one.
- Yeah, me, too.

00:18:32.844 --> 00:18:37.249 line:0%
When I first came to Korea,
there was this group
on Facebook called BSSK.

00:18:37.282 --> 00:18:40.385 align:start size:88% position:13%
- I know what you mean!
- Brothas and Sistas
  of South Korea.

00:18:40.418 --> 00:18:42.420 line:0%
That was so dramatic.
I know what you mean.

00:18:42.453 --> 00:18:44.422 line:0%
As a Black person,
you come to Korea,

00:18:44.455 --> 00:18:46.358 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- that's the first group
  you need to join, right?
- Yes.

00:18:46.391 --> 00:18:48.260
However, as an African,
I realized

00:18:48.293 --> 00:18:50.629
that there are several
African communities here.

00:18:50.662 --> 00:18:52.297 align:start size:91% position:9%
- Yes.
- And they stick together.

00:18:52.330 --> 00:18:54.833 align:start size:75% position:25%
- It's separate!
- Right.

00:18:54.866 --> 00:18:57.269 line:0%
So, it's kind of like
the American--

00:18:57.302 --> 00:19:00.839 align:start size:97% position:3% line:0%
- the Black Americans
  kind of stick to themselves.
- Right, right.

00:19:00.872 --> 00:19:04.276 line:0%
I can't relate, and also
I think have a little residual,

00:19:04.309 --> 00:19:07.612 line:0%
I guess trauma from being back
in the States

00:19:07.645 --> 00:19:09.714
where I was called things
like "Oreo"

00:19:09.747 --> 00:19:12.184
and the other Black girls
hated me.

00:19:12.217 --> 00:19:13.585
And then when you come
to Korea,

00:19:13.618 --> 00:19:16.388
and I'm not even
in the same job sphere--

00:19:16.421 --> 00:19:19.758
yeah, the community altogether
was a little lost for me.

00:19:19.791 --> 00:19:22.294 line:0%
I didn't even know there was
Black community in Korea,

00:19:22.327 --> 00:19:25.730 line:0%
and for the longest time,
I felt alone.

00:19:25.763 --> 00:19:28.466
I expect there to be
some type of connection,

00:19:28.499 --> 00:19:31.803
but when I actually speak
to any Black person

00:19:31.836 --> 00:19:35.540
I feel more of a rival feeling
than I do of a community.

00:19:35.573 --> 00:19:38.944
I'm like,
I thought we're supposed
to help each other out,

00:19:38.977 --> 00:19:40.979
but I never found the community.

00:19:41.012 --> 00:19:42.480
This is the strongest
community I ever found.

00:19:42.513 --> 00:19:43.982
- Mm.
- 'Ey!

00:19:44.015 --> 00:19:45.850
And y'all probably change
when the cameras go off.

00:19:45.883 --> 00:19:49.621 align:start size:97% position:3%
- ( laughs ) No!
-<i> I go by the name of Man1ac.</i>

00:19:49.654 --> 00:19:52.424 line:0%
I was actually born
in Germany. Neuenburg.

00:19:52.457 --> 00:19:53.858 line:0%
My father was in the military.

00:19:53.891 --> 00:19:57.796
<i> And then we got out
 to Korea in 1983.</i>

00:19:57.829 --> 00:19:59.497
You know,
this is the first time

00:19:59.530 --> 00:20:02.334
we spent Chuseok together for--
after Mom passed away.

00:20:02.367 --> 00:20:05.470
It sure is.
And, you know, actually,
I really miss that.

00:20:05.503 --> 00:20:08.273
<i> My father,
 he's fully African-American.</i>

00:20:08.306 --> 00:20:09.674
<i> My mother was Korean.</i>

00:20:09.707 --> 00:20:12.477
<i> She had to raise
 bicultural children,</i>

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:13.745
and I know
it wasn't easy on her,

00:20:13.778 --> 00:20:16.514
<i> and now I have a daughter.</i>

00:20:16.547 --> 00:20:19.484
<i> Lydia definitely doesn't
 think that she's Black.</i>

00:20:19.517 --> 00:20:22.821
But on the other side,
she doesn't think
she's Korean either, so...

00:20:22.854 --> 00:20:24.856
Man1ac: Why do you come home
crying sometimes?

00:20:24.889 --> 00:20:26.691
You say, like,
they don't treat you right.

00:20:26.724 --> 00:20:29.027
Every time
if I go to dance class,

00:20:29.060 --> 00:20:32.364 line:0%
they're not treating
others equally.

00:20:32.397 --> 00:20:34.032 line:0%
Man1ac: Yeah,
they don't treat you equal.

00:20:34.065 --> 00:20:35.900
I felt that
when I was younger, too.

00:20:35.933 --> 00:20:38.637
I remember the times
you came home crying, too,

00:20:38.670 --> 00:20:40.705
- when you were younger.
- Don't put that. Nah.

00:20:40.738 --> 00:20:42.874 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- Nah, I did? For real?
- Yes.

00:20:42.907 --> 00:20:44.709 line:0%
- Because of a--
- What was it over?

00:20:44.742 --> 00:20:46.945 line:0%
I'm not-- I know
I got my feelings hurt a lot,

00:20:46.978 --> 00:20:48.346
I just don't remember
everything.

00:20:48.379 --> 00:20:49.714
There were
so many times where--

00:20:49.747 --> 00:20:51.549
You never really
told me exactly why.

00:20:51.582 --> 00:20:53.018 align:start size:94% position:6%
- Ah.
- But then I know that was--

00:20:53.051 --> 00:20:54.853
I mean, I'm sure
that's what it was.

00:20:54.886 --> 00:20:58.056
Man1ac:
<i> Me looking different,</i>

00:20:58.089 --> 00:20:59.891
they'll let you know
in the playgrounds

00:20:59.924 --> 00:21:02.494
that you're not the same color.

00:21:02.527 --> 00:21:05.930
But I wish I didn't
speak Korean sometimes
because a lot of my problems

00:21:05.963 --> 00:21:07.899
were when I could hear
what they were saying

00:21:07.932 --> 00:21:10.035
and I couldn't
do nothing about it.

00:21:10.068 --> 00:21:14.906
<i> I try to help Lydia navigate
 through all these problems</i>

00:21:14.939 --> 00:21:18.410
<i> by explaining to her
 that she's not the only one
 that's been through it...</i>

00:21:18.443 --> 00:21:20.812
Okay, all right, all right.

00:21:20.845 --> 00:21:22.914
<i> ...and the feelings that
 she's feeling are natural.</i>

00:21:22.947 --> 00:21:25.583
And one day,
she'll get used to it,

00:21:25.616 --> 00:21:30.088
and she'll understand that
it's just a culture difference
and it's not her.

00:21:30.121 --> 00:21:32.991 align:start size:81% position:19%
- All right, Lydia!
- Wow.

00:21:33.024 --> 00:21:35.294
I'm the only one
not catching anything.
Hold on.

00:21:42.633 --> 00:21:44.602 line:0%
Man: Three, two, one.

00:21:44.635 --> 00:21:48.440 line:0%
( humming )

00:21:48.473 --> 00:21:50.442 align:start size:91% position:9% line:0%
- Oh, yay! I have friends.
- Oh!

00:21:50.475 --> 00:21:52.444 line:0%
I really thought I was
going to be the only one.

00:21:52.477 --> 00:21:54.479 line:0%
Oh. Fascinating.

00:21:54.512 --> 00:21:59.584
( speaking Korean )
Because this is the country
I was born in.

00:21:59.617 --> 00:22:02.520
Also because when I'm in Korea,
I feel a lot of comfort

00:22:02.553 --> 00:22:05.957
emotionally and mentally.

00:22:05.990 --> 00:22:10.762
I've been to a lot
of countries, and honestly,

00:22:10.795 --> 00:22:14.065
is there a country out there
as convenient as Korea?

00:22:14.098 --> 00:22:17.902
And the night life here
is great.

00:22:17.935 --> 00:22:22.140
And you can run errands
late at night as during the day.

00:22:22.173 --> 00:22:26.044
It makes me think,
"Is there somewhere
more convenient?"

00:22:26.077 --> 00:22:28.847
I don't think so.

00:22:28.880 --> 00:22:32.417 line:0%
I feel like I'm making ways
for myself to stay here longer,

00:22:32.450 --> 00:22:37.489
but I'm not always sure
if that's going to be
feasible or not,

00:22:37.522 --> 00:22:39.858 align:start size:84% position:16%
- because things
  are always changing.
- Changing, right.

00:22:39.891 --> 00:22:42.994 line:0%
As for me, I always joke,
I will be here until they
kick me out of the country

00:22:43.027 --> 00:22:44.929
because-- I mean,
I know I'm always going to
be the foreigner.

00:22:44.962 --> 00:22:46.498
So they could, you know--
( grunts )

00:22:46.531 --> 00:22:48.967
any time they wanted to,
but me, myself, and I,

00:22:49.000 --> 00:22:51.970
and my partner, you know,
have a pretty nice life
here in Korea.

00:22:52.003 --> 00:22:53.838 line:0%
One thing that
I really love about Korea

00:22:53.871 --> 00:22:56.141 line:0%
and that would keep me here
is the safety.

00:22:56.174 --> 00:22:59.144
Right? 'Cause, you know,
you can leave your
cell phone on the table.

00:22:59.177 --> 00:23:03.047 align:start size:81% position:19%
- You can leave your
  computer there.
- Not around me.

00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:07.152 align:start size:94% position:6%
- No one's going to take it.
- I could stay here
  as long as I want,

00:23:07.185 --> 00:23:12.023 line:0%
but it might be because
I'm a very emotional person

00:23:12.056 --> 00:23:15.126 line:0%
and I was emotionally
damaged when I was a child
by the culture,

00:23:15.159 --> 00:23:17.662
but I love Korea.

00:23:17.695 --> 00:23:19.531
I mean,
I'd have to go with you.

00:23:19.564 --> 00:23:20.632
Because of my children.

00:23:20.665 --> 00:23:23.535
Safety, no guns--

00:23:23.568 --> 00:23:24.936
'cause I had
a lot of friends that--

00:23:24.969 --> 00:23:27.172
that got did in
in the States with guns,

00:23:27.205 --> 00:23:30.041
and that's something
I just don't want to have
to worry about on the side.

00:23:30.074 --> 00:23:33.745
You know what I'm saying?
And I feel safe sending them
to the corner store

00:23:33.778 --> 00:23:36.514 align:start size:91% position:9%
- without anything popping
  off in between, so...
- Right, right.

00:23:36.547 --> 00:23:39.184
...I'd have to go with safety.

00:23:39.217 --> 00:23:41.786
I don't know,
I thought the funniest part

00:23:41.819 --> 00:23:44.823
was when Hyun Min misunderstood
the dating question.

00:23:44.856 --> 00:23:46.157
He was like, "Oh, wait,
we're not talking about friends?

00:23:46.190 --> 00:23:48.993
Oh, never mind.
Let me run back over here."

00:23:49.026 --> 00:23:53.665
Knowing your experiences
makes me look at Korea
in a different kind of way.

00:23:53.698 --> 00:23:56.201
You know, more positive,
and that you, too,

00:23:56.234 --> 00:23:58.770
also experienced
what we experienced, so...

00:23:58.803 --> 00:24:01.039
<i> I'm really thankful
 to be here in Korea,</i>

00:24:01.072 --> 00:24:04.742
but living here
for as long as I have,

00:24:04.775 --> 00:24:06.911
I start to miss my family.

00:24:06.944 --> 00:24:09.214
You know, you start feeling
homesick after a while.

00:24:09.247 --> 00:24:12.617
So, yeah, I'm kind of stuck
in this weird nexus.

00:24:12.650 --> 00:24:15.720
You guys helped me shed a light
on a different perspective.

00:24:15.753 --> 00:24:17.755
I learned so much
from you guys today.

00:24:17.788 --> 00:24:21.159
As a Black woman,
I feel as time progresses,

00:24:21.192 --> 00:24:24.596
<i> there are people
 who are willing to take
 chances with me,</i>

00:24:24.629 --> 00:24:27.899
and I appreciate them.

00:24:27.932 --> 00:24:29.634
Were you, like, careful
with some of the answers

00:24:29.667 --> 00:24:32.070
because of
the negative feedback you got

00:24:32.103 --> 00:24:34.272
from being yourself in Korea?

00:24:34.305 --> 00:24:38.042
I stopped thinking about it
and just went with my gut,

00:24:38.075 --> 00:24:40.111
and my truth, and what
I wanted to say, yeah.

00:24:40.144 --> 00:24:42.947
I went with the truth
and I hope it doesn't X me
out of the game.

00:24:42.980 --> 00:24:46.017 align:start size:81% position:19%
- But, hey.
-<i> Being an outsider
 to both worlds</i>

00:24:46.050 --> 00:24:48.820
is very traumatizing
as a child.

00:24:48.853 --> 00:24:51.189
<i> But I guess I always felt
 a little different,</i>

00:24:51.222 --> 00:24:54.058
but I made that my plus
and ran with that.

00:24:54.091 --> 00:24:56.261
( speaking Korean )
Who is the youngest person here?

00:24:56.294 --> 00:25:01.232
( speaking Korean )
Youngest! Youngest! Youngest!

00:25:01.265 --> 00:25:04.569
( speaking Korean )
It would be good to have
a Black community in Korea,

00:25:04.602 --> 00:25:10.542
because the number
of Black people is quite
limited in Korea...

00:25:10.575 --> 00:25:14.279
( cheering )

00:25:14.312 --> 00:25:18.983
( speaking Korean )
...and it would be nice
to share our common culture

00:25:19.016 --> 00:25:22.887
and make efforts
to tackle racism.

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:24.255 align:start size:84% position:16%
- ( speaking Korean )
  New beginnings!
- New community.

00:25:24.288 --> 00:25:25.723
Man1ac: New community.

00:25:25.756 --> 00:25:28.092
New adventures
with our new friends.

00:25:28.125 --> 00:25:34.132
( speaking Korean )
Cheers!

00:25:39.303 --> 00:25:42.240 line:0%
At the end of the day,
I am always judged
based on my color.

00:25:44.175 --> 00:25:45.777 line:0%
Like, I've been blessed
with this kind of hair.

00:25:45.810 --> 00:25:47.245 line:0%
What do you mean blessed though?

00:25:49.714 --> 00:25:53.718 line:0%
I had that in me that,
"I'm Black, I'm not beautiful,
no one will date me."

00:25:53.751 --> 00:25:55.687 line:0%
Doesn't seem like
I'm really desirable as,

00:25:55.720 --> 00:25:58.856 line:0%
like, my white friends
are more desirable.

00:25:58.889 --> 00:26:01.059 line:0%
- So you find it easy
  to date an Indian.
- It's easy to date.

00:26:01.092 --> 00:26:04.930 align:start size:88% position:13% line:0%
- Yeah, of course.
- Oh, wow. Tell me more.

00:26:08.366 --> 00:26:12.036 align:start size:94% position:6% line:0%
- You guys touched my heart.
- Oh!

00:26:12.069 --> 00:26:13.238 line:0%
You touched my heart.

